# A programmable Boolean function machine -- DRAFT.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.
#
# IMPORTANT: this demo is a design sketch, not a runnable script. It contains no
# commands, only the derivation of a specification that has not been written out
# or executed yet. Read it as notes towards a larger interpreter example.
#
# The design follows the FPGA -- field programmable gate array -- model, see
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Field-programmable_gate_array. An FPGA has an
# array of gates that computes something, memory holding intermediate results,
# inputs and outputs, and a way to reprogram the array.
#
# Here the same structure computes Boolean functions rather than output bits, so
# call it a programmable Boolean function. What gets reprogrammed at run time is
# the function itself, supplied as coefficients on the input streams.
#
# Two simplifications keep the specification small: the programmable function
# takes two variables, and the memory holds a single Boolean algebra element.
# Both generalize in the obvious way, at the cost of more coefficients.
#
# The function is programmed by carrying its coefficients on output streams, one
# per term. Those outputs hold the current program; the input streams carry the
# next one. An update signal decides, at each step, whether the outputs take the
# new coefficients from the inputs or keep the ones they already have. The memory
# is programmed the same way.
#
# Expressions are given in algebraic normal form, which is why the coefficients
# are indexed by bit patterns: one coefficient per monomial. Note that the
# exclusive or of algebraic normal form is written ^ -- + is bitvector addition
# and does not apply here (see demo_1.1 and demo_2.3).
#
# The streams are then:
#
# i0            the update signal
#
# i1000..i1111  the coefficients of the function to compute from the next step
#               onwards, if the update signal is set. The function takes three
#               arguments -- the two inputs and the memory -- hence eight
#               coefficients, indexed by the three bits after `i1`.
#
# i200..i211    the coefficients of the function computing what to store in
#               memory from the next step onwards, if the update signal is set.
#               That function takes the two inputs, hence four coefficients.
#
# i30, i31      the two input values the function is applied to
#
# and the outputs:
#
# o1000..o1111  the coefficients of the function currently being computed
#
# o200..o211    the coefficients of the function currently computing the memory
#
# o3            the value of the current function, applied to the current inputs
#
# o4            the value currently held in memory
#
# Let us build the specification piece by piece.
#
# First the evaluation of the programmed function. Its value depends on the
# coefficients, the two inputs, and the memory as it stood at the previous step.
# In algebraic normal form, with & binding tighter than ^:
#
#   o3[t] = o1000[t-1]
#         ^ o1001[t-1] & o4[t-1]
#         ^ o1010[t-1] & i31[t-1]
#         ^ ...
#         ^ o1111[t-1] & i30[t-1] & i31[t-1] & o4[t-1]
#
# Then the memory, whose function takes only the two inputs:
#
#   o4[t] = o200[t-1]
#         ^ o201[t-1] & i31[t-1]
#         ^ o210[t-1] & i30[t-1]
#         ^ o211[t-1] & i30[t-1] & i31[t-1]
#
# Next, reprogramming. When the update signal is set, every coefficient takes
# the value on its input stream:
#
#   (i0[t] = 1) -> (o1000[t] = i1000[t] && ... && o1111[t] = i1111[t])
#   (i0[t] = 1) -> (o200[t]  = i200[t]  && ... && o211[t]  = i211[t])
#
# and when it is not set, every coefficient keeps the value it had:
#
#   (i0[t] != 1) -> (o1000[t] = o1000[t-1] && ... && o1111[t] = o1111[t-1])
#   (i0[t] != 1) -> (o200[t]  = o200[t-1]  && ... && o211[t]  = o211[t-1])
#
# Note that the update signal is compared against 1, the top element of the
# Boolean algebra, rather than against T: the streams carry Boolean algebra
# elements, so i0[t] is a term and `i0[t] = 1` is the formula asserting it is
# set. T and F are formula constants and would not typecheck here.
#
# The whole specification is the conjunction of those four groups with the two
# evaluation equations, asserted at every time point. Initial conditions at t=0
# have to pin down the starting program and the starting memory, since the
# equations above all refer to t-1.
#
# What remains to turn this into a working demo:
#
#   - write the eight and four coefficient equations out in full, since the
#     ellipses above stand for terms that have to be spelled out,
#   - choose a Boolean algebra for the streams and declare them, as in
#     demo_3.1-interpreter_sbf.tau,
#   - give the initial conditions at t=0,
#   - supply an input file driving a few reprogramming steps, so the run shows
#     the machine computing one function, being reprogrammed, and then computing
#     another.
